<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Records of the Grand Historian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 91 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Shiji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Records of the Grand Historian, or Shiji, is a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from the legendary Yellow Emperor to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a>, completed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> around 91 BCE on the work of his father Sima Tan. Its annals, tables, treatises and biographies became the model for later dynastic histories, and it is the main written source for early states such as the Xia and for frontier peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghu">Donghu</a> and Yelang.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian.jpg" alt="Records of the Grand Historian" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zhonghua Book Company · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8F%B2%E8%AE%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the <b>Shiji</b>, Feizi, said to descend from the legendary adviser Gao Yao, was granted the settlement of Qin, in modern Qingshui County, Gansu, in the 9th century BCE, and in 897 BCE the area became a dependency for raising horses.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to traditional histories compiled more than a millennium later, above all the <b>Shiji</b> of Sima Qian, the Shang dynasty was founded when Tang of Shang, a lord of the eastern plain, overthrew Jie, the tyrannical last ruler of the Xia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the <b>Shiji</b>, King Cheng of Zhou enfeoffed Yu Xiong's great-grandson Xiong Yi as a viscount at Danyang, in the hills of the Han River region; the location of Danyang is disputed, and the Chu ju, a bamboo text among the Tsinghua University manuscripts, gives a different…</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the <b>Shiji</b>, King Wu of Zhou enfeoffed his leading general and adviser Jiang Ziya, known as Lord Taigong, with land in the north of the Shandong peninsula, where he established his capital at Yingqiu, traditionally identified with Linzi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Shiji</b> records that 177 people followed Duke Mu of Qin into death in 621 BCE; a poem in the Book of Songs, 'Yellow Bird', laments three brothers among them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The story in the <b>Shiji</b> that You had lost the trust of his lords by lighting the warning beacons to amuse Bao Si is a later legend.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
